explanation:
Read this example from top to bottom. A calculation such as `savedCoins - paidCoins` is an expression: code that produces a value.
```javascript
const savedCoins = 30;
const paidCoins = 8;
const coinsLeft = savedCoins - paidCoins;
console.log(coinsLeft);
```
The first two lines name the input numbers. The third reads those numbers, subtracts 8 from 30, and assigns 22 to `coinsLeft`. The final line displays 22. It does not subtract again or change the stored values.

Try another complete program with different inputs.
```javascript
const savedCoins = 30;
const paidCoins = 4;
const coinsLeft = savedCoins - paidCoins;
console.log(coinsLeft);
console.log(savedCoins);
```
It displays 26, then 30 on a new line. Changing the spending changes the calculated balance. The calculation leaves `savedCoins` alone. Editing a declaration before rerunning is allowed; trying to reassign a `const` name while the program runs causes an error.

