explanation:
Here is a complete score report.
```javascript
const player = { name: "Zoe", score: 27 };
const { name, score } = player;
const report = `${name} earned ${score} stars.`;
console.log(report);
console.log(score + 3);
```
It prints `Zoe earned 27 stars.` and then 30. The second line reads the two properties. The third creates a string with inserted values. The final arithmetic proves that score remains a number even though it appeared inside a sentence.

An inserted expression can calculate a value too.
```javascript
const player = { name: "Zoe", score: 27 };
const { name, score } = player;
const report = `${name} would have ${score + 3} stars.`;
console.log(report);
console.log("Score: ${score}");
```
The first output is `Zoe would have 30 stars.`. The second is literally `Score: ${score}` because ordinary double quotes do not activate template insertion. The stored score is still 27; calculation inside the sentence has not reassigned it.

