lessons.solution:
function positiveBeforeStop(values) {
  const result = [];
  for (const value of values) {
    if (value === 0) break;
    if (value < 0) continue;
    result.push(value);
  }
  return result;
}
function oneVisit(limit) {
  let count = 0;
  do { count += 1; } while (count < limit);
  return count;
}

explanation:
This standalone example shows the first two steps.
```javascript
const minutes = [10, 30, 40];
const kept = minutes.filter(m => m >= 30);
const seconds = kept.map(m => m * 60);
console.log(kept.join(","));
console.log(seconds.join(","));
console.log(minutes.join(","));
```
The lines print `30,40`, `1800,2400`, and `10,30,40`. Filter keeps entries for which its comparison is true. Map multiplies each kept number by 60. Both return new arrays, so the source list is still available.

A reusable summary handles the empty case explicitly.
```javascript
function summary(minutes) {
  const seconds = minutes.filter(m => m >= 30).map(m => m * 60);
  const total = seconds.reduce((sum, x) => sum + x, 0);
  const average = seconds.length ? total / seconds.length : null;
  return { total, average };
}
console.log(summary([10, 30, 40]).total);
console.log(summary([10, 30, 40]).average);
console.log(summary([]).average);
```
It displays 4200, 2100, and null. The dot reads a property of the returned object. `{ total, average }` is short for an object with properties named total and average holding those variables' values.

