explanation:
Run this script after that markup exists.
```javascript
function addTitle(title) {
  const item = document.createElement("li");
  item.textContent = title;
  document.querySelector("#books").append(item);
}
function removeTitle(title) {
  const items = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll("#books li"));
  const item = items.find(li => li.textContent === title);
  if (item) {
    item.remove();
  }
}
addTitle("Garden Notes");
removeTitle("River Tales");
```
The books list ends with only Garden Notes. The other list still contains Leave this here. Adding increases the first list's count from one to two; removing its original entry returns the count to one.

The `if (item)` guard checks whether an item was found before calling remove. An element value counts as true; undefined counts as false. This avoids an error for a title that is absent. `.remove()` detaches only that element. Do not clear the entire parent list to remove one book. When titles repeat, this implementation removes the first matching item rather than all duplicates.

For another complete worked example, start with the same HTML in a fresh page and run this script after the lists exist:
```javascript
const books = document.querySelector("#books");
const added = document.createElement("li");
added.textContent = "Weather Notes";
books.append(added);
console.log(document.querySelectorAll("#books li").length);
