explanation:
Here is a complete small BFS example.
```javascript
const graph = { P: ["Q", "R"], Q: ["S"], R: ["S"], S: [] };
function search(start, target) {
  const queue = [[start, [start]]];
  const seen = new Set([start]);
  let visited = 0;
  while (queue.length) {
    const [node, path] = queue.shift();
    visited++;
    if (node === target) {
      return { path, visited };
    }
    for (const next of graph[node] || []) {
      if (!seen.has(next)) {
        seen.add(next);
        queue.push([next, [...path, next]]);
      }
    }
  }
  return { path: null, visited };
}
const result = search("P", "S");
console.log(result.path.join("->"), result.visited);
```
This prints `P->Q->S 4`. The queue order is P, Q, R, S. S is recorded when first discovered from Q, so discovering it again from R does not add another visit.

DFS, depth-first search, follows one branch deeply before trying another. A stack removes the newest entry first; an array's pop does that. Here is a separate complete traversal example, with a cycle handled by seen.
```javascript
const graph = { P: ["Q", "R"], Q: ["S"], R: [], S: ["P"] };
function depthVisits(start) {
  const stack = [start];
  const seen = new Set();
  const order = [];
  while (stack.length) {
    const node = stack.pop();
    if (seen.has(node)) {
      continue;
    }
    seen.add(node);
    order.push(node);
    for (const next of [...(graph[node] || [])].reverse()) {
      stack.push(next);
    }
  }
  return order;
}
console.log(depthVisits("P").join(","));
```
It prints `P,Q,S,R`. Continue skips the rest of the current iteration. Copying and reversing neighbors makes the first listed neighbor processed first by this stack. DFS does not generally promise the shortest path.

